1
00:00:47,213 --> 00:00:54,179
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

2
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:56,473
JONGNO PAWNSHOP

3
00:00:56,973 --> 00:00:57,807
EPISODE 3

4
00:00:57,891 --> 00:00:59,225
What, a pickpocket?

5
00:00:59,976 --> 00:01:02,145
I have no idea what you're talking about.

6
00:01:02,228 --> 00:01:05,398
That's right. She's my customer.

7
00:01:08,109 --> 00:01:08,943
Here.

8
00:01:09,027 --> 00:01:10,987
She came to pawn this.

9
00:01:14,866 --> 00:01:15,825
Then what is this?

10
00:01:16,993 --> 00:01:19,496
My father gave me a wallet
for my graduation gift.

11
00:01:19,579 --> 00:01:21,664
It looks just like that wallet.

12
00:01:21,748 --> 00:01:22,582
Let me see.

13
00:01:24,084 --> 00:01:25,251
There's some writing on the wallet.

14
00:01:25,919 --> 00:01:28,713
"Sato Hideo."

15
00:01:28,797 --> 00:01:30,548
That name sounds familiar.

16
00:01:30,632 --> 00:01:31,841
What do you think?

17
00:01:33,718 --> 00:01:36,179
Did you say you were looking
for Jongno Pawnshop?

18
00:01:36,930 --> 00:01:39,933
That's too bad.
This isn't the Jongno Pawnshop.

19
00:01:40,016 --> 00:01:42,102
Stop lying. I'm not falling for it again.

20
00:01:42,185 --> 00:01:45,063
I'm telling you, it's not.
Go check the sign outside.

21
00:01:45,146 --> 00:01:46,606
I did see the sign outside.

22
00:01:46,689 --> 00:01:48,691
You're trying to get me out
so you can run away.

23
00:01:48,775 --> 00:01:49,901
You think I don't know that?

24
00:01:49,984 --> 00:01:52,112
I'm not lying. This is…

25
00:01:52,195 --> 00:01:53,655
THE FIRST JONGNO PAWNSHOP

26
00:01:53,738 --> 00:01:54,614
"The First."

27
00:01:54,697 --> 00:01:57,075
This is "The First Jongno Pawnshop."

28
00:01:57,158 --> 00:01:59,077
Though it opened only three years ago.

29
00:01:59,160 --> 00:01:59,994
That's right.

30
00:02:00,078 --> 00:02:03,039
It's the Jongno Pawnshop
with "The First" in its name.

31
00:02:03,873 --> 00:02:07,085
Do you know how many pawnshops
there are around Jongno?

32
00:02:07,168 --> 00:02:08,253
There must be dozens.

33
00:02:08,336 --> 00:02:10,588
There are probably more than ten
just down the alley--

34
00:02:11,965 --> 00:02:14,217
I don't care
if this is the first one or not.

35
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:16,010
Enough with the lies.

36
00:02:19,013 --> 00:02:20,682
I'll give you one last chance.

37
00:02:21,474 --> 00:02:23,101
Give it to me while I'm being nice.

38
00:02:27,647 --> 00:02:30,733
Honestly, I don't know
what you want from me.

39
00:02:30,817 --> 00:02:32,777
I said this is your last chance.

40
00:02:34,028 --> 00:02:35,738
Hands in the air!

41
00:02:38,199 --> 00:02:39,909
Don't move! Hands up!

42
00:02:41,202 --> 00:02:42,871
Move! Bring everyone down.

43
00:02:45,081 --> 00:02:46,124
Damn it.

44
00:02:47,083 --> 00:02:49,002
I can't believe
you called the police over this.

45
00:02:49,085 --> 00:02:51,171
Being the inspector general's son
must be nice.

46
00:02:52,672 --> 00:02:53,923
Why are your hands up?

47
00:02:54,507 --> 00:02:55,466
I didn't call them.

48
00:02:55,550 --> 00:02:56,718
You there.

49
00:02:57,635 --> 00:02:58,469
Quiet!

50
00:03:00,346 --> 00:03:03,474
I'm Sato Hideo, an interpreter trainee
at the Government-General of Joseon.

51
00:03:03,558 --> 00:03:04,767
Freeze.

52
00:03:06,811 --> 00:03:09,731
I was going to show you
my Government-General ID.

53
00:03:17,697 --> 00:03:19,908
An interpreter trainee
for the Government-General?

54
00:03:19,991 --> 00:03:21,451
Yes, my name is Sato Hideo.

55
00:03:23,912 --> 00:03:26,122
I didn't want to mention this,

56
00:03:26,623 --> 00:03:29,375
but Sato Shinichi,
the inspector general, is my father.

57
00:03:30,251 --> 00:03:31,085
That's right.

58
00:03:32,170 --> 00:03:33,796
I saw him at Keijo Station.

59
00:03:36,257 --> 00:03:38,384
Forgive me, I didn't realize who you were!

60
00:03:39,093 --> 00:03:40,637
Come on. Put the guns down!

61
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:42,138
- Yes, sir. Sorry!
- I'm sorry!

62
00:03:44,224 --> 00:03:45,141
It's fine.

63
00:03:46,517 --> 00:03:49,103
You sure know
how to use your father's influence.

64
00:03:49,187 --> 00:03:51,522
It really is nice
being the inspector general's son.

65
00:03:55,109 --> 00:03:56,778
Anyway, what's going on?

66
00:03:56,861 --> 00:04:00,240
We're looking for the Gugukdan members
who targeted your father yesterday.

67
00:04:00,323 --> 00:04:02,492
I was there myself.

68
00:04:02,575 --> 00:04:03,993
By the Gugukdan,

69
00:04:04,077 --> 00:04:06,621
do you mean the organization
based in Shanghai?

70
00:04:06,704 --> 00:04:08,289
Was that man with the Gugukdan?

71
00:04:08,373 --> 00:04:09,540
Yes.

72
00:04:09,624 --> 00:04:12,085
We got a tip-off that a Gugukdan member
recently came to Keijo

73
00:04:12,168 --> 00:04:14,295
and headed for Jongno, so we're after him.

74
00:04:15,546 --> 00:04:16,881
Are you the innkeeper?

75
00:04:16,965 --> 00:04:19,342
If you're hiding someone
from the Gugukdan, tell me now!

76
00:04:19,425 --> 00:04:21,636
Wait, wait. Shoot<i>…</i>

77
00:04:21,719 --> 00:04:23,388
I mean, don't shoot.

78
00:04:23,471 --> 00:04:25,056
Wait a moment. What's he saying?

79
00:04:27,141 --> 00:04:28,101
I can interpret.

80
00:04:31,229 --> 00:04:32,063
So…

81
00:04:32,647 --> 00:04:36,192
He's asking if you're hiding
a Gugukdan independence fighter here.

82
00:04:36,276 --> 00:04:38,820
An independence fighter?
That's ridiculous.

83
00:04:38,903 --> 00:04:40,071
Absolutely not.

84
00:04:40,154 --> 00:04:41,489
This inn…

85
00:04:41,572 --> 00:04:42,949
This place…

86
00:04:43,032 --> 00:04:44,909
…has no ties to the independence movement.

87
00:04:44,993 --> 00:04:46,536
…isn't tied to the movement.

88
00:04:46,619 --> 00:04:49,038
This place is the…

89
00:04:49,122 --> 00:04:51,624
Right, it's the watermill in Jongno.

90
00:04:52,375 --> 00:04:53,501
It's the watermill.

91
00:04:53,584 --> 00:04:55,128
What are you babbling about? Mill?

92
00:04:55,211 --> 00:04:56,504
"Mill"?

93
00:04:56,587 --> 00:04:59,257
Yes, a watermill. Do they know
what that means around here?

94
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:01,759
- Translate that for me. I'll explain.
- What?

95
00:05:01,843 --> 00:05:05,722
A watermill is where the wheel
starts turning, and the pounding begins--

96
00:05:05,805 --> 00:05:07,181
Hold on!

97
00:05:07,265 --> 00:05:08,766
I'll explain it myself.

98
00:05:08,850 --> 00:05:09,767
Well…

99
00:05:09,851 --> 00:05:10,977
This inn is a place

100
00:05:12,103 --> 00:05:16,149
mostly used by young men and women
during the day.

101
00:05:16,232 --> 00:05:18,026
What do they do in broad daylight?

102
00:05:18,109 --> 00:05:19,777
Are independence fighters hiding here?

103
00:05:19,861 --> 00:05:21,029
No, that's not it!

104
00:05:21,112 --> 00:05:22,030
What?

105
00:05:22,113 --> 00:05:23,823
Don't they have watermills in Japan?

106
00:05:23,906 --> 00:05:24,866
This is where…

107
00:05:27,577 --> 00:05:30,830
people come in pairs to play sports.

108
00:05:30,913 --> 00:05:31,873
What?

109
00:05:32,582 --> 00:05:34,667
Sports in pairs?

110
00:05:34,751 --> 00:05:36,836
Yes, sports.

111
00:05:44,969 --> 00:05:45,970
I get it.

112
00:05:49,015 --> 00:05:51,351
Still, check their IDs. Search the place.

113
00:05:51,434 --> 00:05:53,061
- Yes, sir!
- They'll check IDs.

114
00:05:53,144 --> 00:05:54,062
What?

115
00:05:55,772 --> 00:05:56,814
Okay.

116
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:03,112
If I may ask, Mr. Sato,
what were you doing here?

117
00:06:04,113 --> 00:06:05,073
Who is she?

118
00:06:08,409 --> 00:06:09,369
She's…

119
00:06:09,452 --> 00:06:10,578
If I get arrested,

120
00:06:11,579 --> 00:06:14,165
you'll never find that pawn ticket.

121
00:06:15,458 --> 00:06:18,002
How did you know it was a pawn ticket?

122
00:06:18,086 --> 00:06:19,670
I never told you what it was.

123
00:06:21,047 --> 00:06:21,964
This woman…

124
00:06:22,048 --> 00:06:24,842
Sin Tae-jin! Who… is that?

125
00:06:25,426 --> 00:06:26,761
The owner of that pawn ticket.

126
00:06:30,765 --> 00:06:33,267
Does the inspector general know him?

127
00:06:33,351 --> 00:06:34,811
I want to know all of a sudden.

128
00:06:36,729 --> 00:06:37,980
This lady…

129
00:06:40,691 --> 00:06:41,692
is here with me.

130
00:06:43,361 --> 00:06:44,237
With you?

131
00:06:45,363 --> 00:06:46,197
That's right.

132
00:06:47,031 --> 00:06:48,282
We're a pair.

133
00:06:49,867 --> 00:06:51,285
She's my partner in sports.

134
00:06:53,663 --> 00:06:55,289
Oh, your "partner in sports."

135
00:06:56,374 --> 00:06:59,502
I'm sorry, I was being slow. My apologies.

136
00:07:00,086 --> 00:07:01,546
- Put the gun down.
- Yes, sir.

137
00:07:01,629 --> 00:07:04,048
What did you just tell them?

138
00:07:04,132 --> 00:07:05,007
Let go, will you?

139
00:07:05,091 --> 00:07:07,635
You'd better cooperate with me for now.

140
00:07:08,302 --> 00:07:09,512
Thank you.

141
00:07:09,595 --> 00:07:11,097
Please keep this brief.

142
00:07:11,180 --> 00:07:12,348
I'm in a bit of a rush.

143
00:07:12,432 --> 00:07:14,100
Yes, sir, I understand.

144
00:07:14,183 --> 00:07:17,103
I'd appreciate it if you kept this

145
00:07:18,438 --> 00:07:19,313
just between us.

146
00:07:19,397 --> 00:07:21,065
Certainly.

147
00:07:21,149 --> 00:07:23,401
We'll wrap up the search quickly.

148
00:07:23,484 --> 00:07:26,362
The first half, the second half,
and even overtime.

149
00:07:26,446 --> 00:07:28,281
I hope you enjoy the whole game!

150
00:07:29,031 --> 00:07:29,949
Hurry up!

151
00:07:31,868 --> 00:07:32,702
See?

152
00:07:35,663 --> 00:07:38,207
Once the search is over,
let's settle our business.

153
00:07:39,125 --> 00:07:39,959
All right?

154
00:07:43,421 --> 00:07:44,672
We caught someone suspicious.

155
00:07:45,673 --> 00:07:47,592
He's outside. Come have a look.

156
00:07:49,385 --> 00:07:50,470
Mr. Sato.

157
00:07:50,553 --> 00:07:53,556
Since you were at the scene last night,
you must have seen the suspect.

158
00:07:53,639 --> 00:07:55,141
Please see if you can identify him.

159
00:07:57,602 --> 00:07:58,478
Sure.

160
00:08:01,189 --> 00:08:02,940
I'll be right back.

161
00:08:03,024 --> 00:08:05,651
Don't even think about running off.

162
00:08:05,735 --> 00:08:07,862
You're smart enough to know
this situation, right?

163
00:08:08,571 --> 00:08:10,531
- Yes.
- Good to know.

164
00:08:11,282 --> 00:08:12,283
If you'll excuse me.

165
00:08:25,213 --> 00:08:26,923
This is driving me crazy, darn it.

166
00:08:29,634 --> 00:08:30,468
Excuse me.

167
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:32,261
Excuse me?

168
00:08:32,845 --> 00:08:35,264
- Yes?
- What's going on here?

169
00:08:36,849 --> 00:08:40,228
Well, last night…

170
00:08:46,067 --> 00:08:47,193
The thing is…

171
00:08:51,989 --> 00:08:52,865
It's not him.

172
00:08:54,158 --> 00:08:56,327
He doesn't even fit the age profile.

173
00:08:56,410 --> 00:08:58,704
- Take him to the Jongno Station.
- Yes, sir!

174
00:09:00,665 --> 00:09:01,999
I told you, it wasn't him.

175
00:09:02,083 --> 00:09:03,000
Take him!

176
00:09:04,418 --> 00:09:06,170
It's suspicious that he ran.

177
00:09:06,254 --> 00:09:07,922
Why would he run if he was innocent?

178
00:09:08,005 --> 00:09:10,299
The Japanese police
were chasing me with guns.

179
00:09:10,383 --> 00:09:12,093
Any Joseon person would be scared!

180
00:09:12,176 --> 00:09:13,302
Shut up!

181
00:09:13,386 --> 00:09:14,887
Wait!

182
00:09:19,225 --> 00:09:20,601
- Go!
- Yes, sir!

183
00:09:24,355 --> 00:09:25,481
You can go on.

184
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:28,234
I'll hold him here.

185
00:09:28,317 --> 00:09:30,319
Then I'll leave him in your hands.

186
00:09:40,621 --> 00:09:41,581
Go on, now.

187
00:09:41,664 --> 00:09:43,749
Are you from Joseon?

188
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:45,334
I'm…

189
00:09:50,923 --> 00:09:52,258
Just go.

190
00:09:52,341 --> 00:09:53,426
Thank you.

191
00:09:55,386 --> 00:09:56,387
Thank you.

192
00:10:11,777 --> 00:10:14,196
THE FIRST JONGNO PAWNSHOP

193
00:10:14,280 --> 00:10:15,281
Seriously?

194
00:10:16,616 --> 00:10:18,409
It really says "The First."

195
00:10:26,834 --> 00:10:27,668
Let's go.

196
00:10:29,545 --> 00:10:30,838
Hold on.

197
00:10:32,381 --> 00:10:33,424
What's going on?

198
00:10:34,091 --> 00:10:36,344
Where's the person who wore this?

199
00:10:38,095 --> 00:10:38,971
Well…

200
00:10:40,097 --> 00:10:41,599
Apparently, some man reported

201
00:10:41,682 --> 00:10:44,143
that his wife was cheating
on him in this inn.

202
00:10:44,226 --> 00:10:46,395
So the police came
to catch them red-handed.

203
00:10:46,479 --> 00:10:49,440
It's absolute chaos.
That man is waiting outside.

204
00:10:49,523 --> 00:10:50,483
So…

205
00:10:51,067 --> 00:10:52,943
did you see what he looked like?

206
00:10:53,027 --> 00:10:54,945
He had these red eyes.

207
00:10:55,488 --> 00:10:59,241
Right, he was asking if anyone had seen
a woman with a red-jeweled ring.

208
00:10:59,325 --> 00:11:00,743
Is she here?

209
00:11:00,826 --> 00:11:01,702
Oh my.

210
00:11:02,703 --> 00:11:06,290
Is there something I can help you with?

211
00:11:08,459 --> 00:11:10,586
She said if we traded clothes,

212
00:11:10,670 --> 00:11:13,673
my husband would follow her instead.

213
00:11:15,007 --> 00:11:15,966
What?

214
00:11:36,195 --> 00:11:38,280
Let's not see each other again.

215
00:11:40,950 --> 00:11:42,993
Wait. It fits perfectly.

216
00:11:43,744 --> 00:11:45,830
Should I keep this instead of selling it?

217
00:12:09,061 --> 00:12:14,191
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

218
00:12:15,276 --> 00:12:16,986
Do you mean that customer from earlier?

219
00:12:18,070 --> 00:12:22,074
How would I know
where all my customers live?

220
00:12:22,158 --> 00:12:23,451
I'm very sorry.

221
00:12:24,535 --> 00:12:25,745
Yes, I understand.

222
00:12:26,662 --> 00:12:28,164
All right. Go on already.

223
00:12:28,748 --> 00:12:31,292
You said you didn't know anything
about Jongno Pawnshop.

224
00:12:32,042 --> 00:12:33,210
Do you happen to know

225
00:12:33,294 --> 00:12:35,296
a person named Sin Tae-jin?

226
00:12:35,379 --> 00:12:36,797
Sin Tae-jin?

227
00:12:36,881 --> 00:12:38,174
Sin Tae-jin…

228
00:12:38,257 --> 00:12:40,217
Sin Tae-jin…

229
00:12:40,801 --> 00:12:42,595
I've never heard that name before.

230
00:12:42,678 --> 00:12:44,388
Who could that be?

231
00:12:45,264 --> 00:12:47,391
Never mind. You have a good day.

232
00:12:47,892 --> 00:12:49,935
All right. You should really get going.

233
00:12:50,019 --> 00:12:53,439
APPRAISAL

234
00:12:56,734 --> 00:12:58,486
That shaved years off my life.

235
00:12:58,569 --> 00:13:00,029
JONGNO PAWNSHOP MAIN BRANCH

236
00:13:00,112 --> 00:13:01,113
JONGNO PAWNSHOP BRANCH 2

237
00:13:02,615 --> 00:13:04,158
JUNGRO PAWNSHOP

238
00:13:05,451 --> 00:13:07,119
ORIGINAL JONGNO PAWNSHOP

239
00:13:07,912 --> 00:13:09,330
NEW JONGNO PAWNSHOP

240
00:13:09,413 --> 00:13:10,956
JONGRU PAWNSHOP

241
00:13:11,040 --> 00:13:12,458
JEONGRO PAWNSHOP

242
00:13:12,541 --> 00:13:14,168
REAL JONGNO PAWNSHOP

243
00:13:16,253 --> 00:13:19,548
So it wasn't a lie.

244
00:13:19,632 --> 00:13:21,634
JONGNO PAWNSHOP

245
00:14:06,762 --> 00:14:08,055
Mother.

246
00:14:26,866 --> 00:14:28,200
Who are you?

247
00:14:30,327 --> 00:14:33,455
I'm Sato Hideo, Mr. Suzuki's colleague
at the Government-General.

248
00:14:34,039 --> 00:14:35,499
Oh, I see.

249
00:14:37,418 --> 00:14:39,962
He has no family.

250
00:14:40,045 --> 00:14:42,047
If anything happens, please contact me.

251
00:14:43,173 --> 00:14:44,341
Please take good care of him.

252
00:14:45,676 --> 00:14:46,552
I will.

253
00:14:51,807 --> 00:14:54,268
It's a great honor to serve you again

254
00:14:54,351 --> 00:14:57,313
as chief of police
of the Government-General of Joseon, sir.

255
00:14:58,939 --> 00:15:01,901
You left for Japan in 1920, didn't you?

256
00:15:02,693 --> 00:15:03,986
I did.

257
00:15:04,737 --> 00:15:06,155
How have you been?

258
00:15:06,238 --> 00:15:08,240
Very well, thanks to you.

259
00:15:10,200 --> 00:15:14,538
Compared to how I was starving
before I came to Joseon back in 1912,

260
00:15:15,205 --> 00:15:16,790
Keijo feels like paradise.

261
00:15:19,126 --> 00:15:20,961
Back in 1912…

262
00:15:21,712 --> 00:15:24,006
You've been in Joseon for 20 years now.

263
00:15:24,548 --> 00:15:25,466
Yes.

264
00:15:25,549 --> 00:15:27,468
Do you remember what you told me

265
00:15:28,427 --> 00:15:31,013
when we first met that year?

266
00:15:32,681 --> 00:15:35,851
"Three years for a stone.
Give Joseon ten."

267
00:15:35,935 --> 00:15:38,354
"As the old saying goes,
even a cold stone warms up

268
00:15:38,437 --> 00:15:39,813
if you sit on it for three years."

269
00:15:40,522 --> 00:15:43,108
"Even if life is hard
in this foreign land,

270
00:15:43,192 --> 00:15:44,401
it's still better

271
00:15:45,069 --> 00:15:48,364
than being treated like dirt and starving
in Japan. Just hold out for ten years."

272
00:15:49,615 --> 00:15:51,575
You told me to keep that in mind.

273
00:15:53,369 --> 00:15:56,163
I've kept those words close to my heart
and made it this far.

274
00:15:56,246 --> 00:15:57,665
I see.

275
00:15:58,582 --> 00:16:00,709
For people like you and me,

276
00:16:01,710 --> 00:16:03,379
Joseon is still

277
00:16:03,462 --> 00:16:06,215
a land of opportunity,
now and for years to come.

278
00:16:07,549 --> 00:16:08,926
- Fukuda.
- Sir.

279
00:16:09,885 --> 00:16:12,304
After 20 years in Joseon,

280
00:16:13,138 --> 00:16:14,556
is chief of police all you want?

281
00:16:16,266 --> 00:16:17,226
Pardon?

282
00:16:26,610 --> 00:16:28,153
You have my absolute loyalty!

283
00:16:28,821 --> 00:16:30,364
Prove your loyalty

284
00:16:31,115 --> 00:16:33,242
by wiping out the Gugukdan.

285
00:16:33,826 --> 00:16:34,743
Yes, sir!

286
00:16:46,588 --> 00:16:50,259
I hardly had any customers,
so I didn't run around much.

287
00:16:50,342 --> 00:16:53,929
But why do my rubber shoes
keep wearing out?

288
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:56,682
Did you barely have any customers today?

289
00:16:57,307 --> 00:16:58,434
No.

290
00:16:58,517 --> 00:17:02,479
There are so many taxis these days,
I can barely get one passenger a day.

291
00:17:02,563 --> 00:17:05,232
They call it the age of speed or whatever.

292
00:17:05,315 --> 00:17:07,401
I managed to get one passenger today,

293
00:17:07,484 --> 00:17:08,652
and he was a traitor.

294
00:17:09,862 --> 00:17:11,947
Some Joseon scum
working at the Government-General.

295
00:17:12,614 --> 00:17:14,033
A Joseon man at the Government-General?

296
00:17:14,116 --> 00:17:16,535
Not a Joseon man. He's Joseon scum.

297
00:17:16,618 --> 00:17:19,163
How could a Joseon person possibly work
at the Government-General?

298
00:17:20,581 --> 00:17:21,582
Good grief.

299
00:17:21,665 --> 00:17:22,708
My back hurts.

300
00:17:23,292 --> 00:17:24,418
Hey, No-ma.

301
00:17:24,501 --> 00:17:27,838
Why don't you try delivering food
instead of pulling a rickshaw?

302
00:17:27,921 --> 00:17:29,840
- A delivery boy?
- Yeah.

303
00:17:29,923 --> 00:17:30,966
On a bicycle?

304
00:17:31,050 --> 00:17:33,302
These days, more and more people
are having things

305
00:17:33,385 --> 00:17:35,137
like <i>seolleongtang</i>
and <i>naengmyeon </i>delivered.

306
00:17:36,221 --> 00:17:38,891
Do you think I can get whatever I want?

307
00:17:38,974 --> 00:17:41,060
Bicycles don't come cheap, you know.

308
00:17:41,643 --> 00:17:44,313
What else can you do?
You said there are no customers.

309
00:17:49,109 --> 00:17:50,027
Are you asleep?

310
00:17:55,240 --> 00:17:57,451
He hasn't made a penny,
yet he's fast asleep.

311
00:18:27,147 --> 00:18:29,858
I almost went to prison
before I made it to America.

312
00:18:39,409 --> 00:18:41,495
What could he have left at the pawnshop?

313
00:18:42,830 --> 00:18:43,705
Hold on.

314
00:18:44,206 --> 00:18:47,167
Sin Tae-jin is a Joseon name.

315
00:18:49,503 --> 00:18:50,546
Unbelievable.

316
00:18:50,629 --> 00:18:53,882
As if taking our country wasn't enough,
they even stole our pawn tickets!

317
00:18:56,677 --> 00:18:57,511
Seriously…

318
00:19:00,347 --> 00:19:02,432
BOUNTY 300 WON

319
00:19:03,267 --> 00:19:05,853
BOUNTY 500 WON

320
00:19:08,021 --> 00:19:11,275
HANSEONG MEDICINE CLINIC

321
00:19:11,358 --> 00:19:13,652
SUSPECT'S DESCRIPTION
NAME: HAN CHEOL-YONG

322
00:19:15,654 --> 00:19:16,989
Please take this.

323
00:19:17,072 --> 00:19:19,408
You should be able to lie low for a while.

324
00:19:20,159 --> 00:19:21,451
Oh dear.

325
00:19:21,535 --> 00:19:22,828
I'm grateful,

326
00:19:22,911 --> 00:19:24,997
but I hate to impose on you like this.

327
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:26,165
You know who I am.

328
00:19:26,748 --> 00:19:29,585
I'm the only daughter
of Joil Textile Factory's president,

329
00:19:29,668 --> 00:19:31,336
Joseon's biggest traitor, and a marquess.

330
00:19:36,091 --> 00:19:38,677
I'm ashamed
that I can't punish my father myself.

331
00:19:39,261 --> 00:19:41,138
So I want to make amends this way.

332
00:19:41,221 --> 00:19:42,055
Thank you.

333
00:19:43,307 --> 00:19:44,892
Lee Young-bok, my father's secretary,

334
00:19:44,975 --> 00:19:47,060
managed to siphon off company funds.

335
00:19:48,103 --> 00:19:51,315
In an emergency,
it's quicker than selling my gold jewelry.

336
00:19:53,692 --> 00:19:54,776
How is Suzuki doing?

337
00:19:54,860 --> 00:19:57,821
The worst has passed,
but he's not out of the woods yet.

338
00:19:58,697 --> 00:20:01,450
Sato Hideo came to visit Suzuki
a few days ago.

339
00:20:02,534 --> 00:20:04,828
He told me to take good care of Suzuki

340
00:20:04,912 --> 00:20:06,371
since he has no family to turn to.

341
00:20:07,247 --> 00:20:09,333
As if he cares about his colleague.

342
00:20:09,917 --> 00:20:11,919
He's Sato Shinichi's son.

343
00:20:12,002 --> 00:20:14,129
He'd shoot us without a second thought.

344
00:20:14,713 --> 00:20:18,258
He's right. He did a fine job
protecting his father that day too.

345
00:20:18,842 --> 00:20:20,886
Then who shot Sato that day?

346
00:20:22,137 --> 00:20:25,265
Why did the Government-General
start coming after us over that?

347
00:20:25,349 --> 00:20:29,061
If we'd killed Sato, I'd understand.
But this feels a little unfair.

348
00:20:30,729 --> 00:20:34,233
Without Suzuki, no one can tell us
what's going on at the Government-General,

349
00:20:34,316 --> 00:20:36,568
let alone Sato's schedule and movements.

350
00:20:38,237 --> 00:20:40,280
PERSONNEL NOTICE
GOVERNMENT-GENERAL INTERPRETER

351
00:20:44,409 --> 00:20:48,956
The interpreter trainee spot
at the Government-General is open.

352
00:20:49,790 --> 00:20:53,502
Sato Hideo, the interpreter trainee,
was moved into Suzuki's position,

353
00:20:53,585 --> 00:20:56,046
leaving the interpreter trainee's post
vacant.

354
00:20:56,129 --> 00:20:57,005
Are you saying

355
00:20:57,881 --> 00:21:00,884
you want to put another spy
in that interpreter trainee post?

356
00:21:00,968 --> 00:21:01,843
Yes.

357
00:21:02,678 --> 00:21:04,930
We need one of our people
in the Government-General.

358
00:21:05,514 --> 00:21:06,848
We all know that.

359
00:21:06,932 --> 00:21:08,725
But how do we get someone in there?

360
00:21:08,809 --> 00:21:10,769
An interpreter trainee
has to speak English.

361
00:21:10,852 --> 00:21:13,021
It's hard to find fluent English speakers.

362
00:21:13,105 --> 00:21:14,231
Is there anyone like that?

363
00:21:15,274 --> 00:21:16,400
There is.

364
00:21:17,025 --> 00:21:18,193
Who is that?

365
00:21:20,028 --> 00:21:22,531
The girl who posed as a live-in maid
at our house.

366
00:21:23,115 --> 00:21:24,616
What? A live-in maid?

367
00:21:24,700 --> 00:21:27,160
She's sharp and a good liar.

368
00:21:27,244 --> 00:21:28,912
She's quick on her feet.

369
00:21:28,996 --> 00:21:30,330
She had the nerve to come

370
00:21:30,414 --> 00:21:32,833
while we were hosting Sato
and pressure us.

371
00:21:32,916 --> 00:21:37,254
And she's quick with her hands,
and her English is surprisingly good.

372
00:21:40,841 --> 00:21:41,675
Right.

373
00:21:41,758 --> 00:21:43,927
I've verified her English skills.

374
00:21:44,011 --> 00:21:46,847
But I'm against sending her in as a spy.

375
00:21:46,930 --> 00:21:48,557
A spy holds not only our lives

376
00:21:49,433 --> 00:21:51,685
but the fate of the Shanghai headquarters
in their hands.

377
00:21:53,020 --> 00:21:54,021
We can't put someone

378
00:21:54,104 --> 00:21:56,481
who doesn't care about independence
in that position.

379
00:21:57,190 --> 00:21:59,484
I'm worried that we just let her go.

380
00:21:59,568 --> 00:22:00,652
Right.

381
00:22:01,153 --> 00:22:04,573
From what I've heard,
she seems to care about nothing but money.

382
00:22:04,656 --> 00:22:07,993
If we send her as a spy,
she could be turned against us with money.

383
00:22:08,076 --> 00:22:10,037
She doesn't care about independence,

384
00:22:10,120 --> 00:22:11,705
so money will sway her either way.

385
00:22:12,289 --> 00:22:13,373
Exactly.

386
00:22:14,374 --> 00:22:16,460
She'll work for whoever offers her money.

387
00:22:17,127 --> 00:22:20,630
But there's something
she wants more than money.

388
00:22:21,757 --> 00:22:22,716
She wants…

389
00:22:23,592 --> 00:22:24,843
to go to America.

390
00:22:25,802 --> 00:22:27,721
What? America?

391
00:22:27,804 --> 00:22:31,433
For her, money is just a means
to get to America.

392
00:22:31,933 --> 00:22:34,269
So if going to America
is part of the deal,

393
00:22:34,353 --> 00:22:36,688
she won't turn down our offer

394
00:22:37,356 --> 00:22:39,024
or betray us.

395
00:22:40,359 --> 00:22:42,361
Why does she want to go to America?

396
00:22:44,946 --> 00:22:46,615
<i>Don't you care about your own country?</i>

397
00:22:46,698 --> 00:22:50,702
Look, I was born into a country
already under Japanese rule.

398
00:22:50,786 --> 00:22:52,496
Is that my fault?

399
00:22:52,579 --> 00:22:54,456
You can't blame me, can you?

400
00:22:54,539 --> 00:22:57,125
Am I supposed to fight for independence
just because I was born in Joseon?

401
00:22:59,294 --> 00:23:01,797
What has Joseon ever done for me?

402
00:23:04,800 --> 00:23:06,718
I don't know her story.

403
00:23:07,302 --> 00:23:10,013
But her drive to leave Joseon

404
00:23:10,097 --> 00:23:13,558
was as strong
as our longing for independence.

405
00:23:14,184 --> 00:23:18,146
I felt that, deep down,
what drove her was hatred.

406
00:23:19,689 --> 00:23:23,318
And hatred
can be just as powerful as love.

407
00:23:24,236 --> 00:23:25,278
Even so,

408
00:23:25,862 --> 00:23:28,323
you don't even know her name
or where she lives.

409
00:23:29,241 --> 00:23:30,867
I may not know where she lives,

410
00:23:31,827 --> 00:23:33,870
but I know where to find her.

411
00:23:44,172 --> 00:23:45,590
I don't see her anywhere.

412
00:23:46,341 --> 00:23:47,843
Oh my!

413
00:23:50,387 --> 00:23:51,471
- Are you okay?
- Sorry.

414
00:23:51,555 --> 00:23:52,722
I'm sorry.

415
00:23:52,806 --> 00:23:53,849
Good grief.

416
00:23:54,641 --> 00:23:55,600
Thank you.

417
00:24:06,695 --> 00:24:07,612
My pocket watch.

418
00:24:08,864 --> 00:24:10,824
Goodness, my wallet's gone too.

419
00:24:14,494 --> 00:24:15,495
FIRST-CLASS COMPARTMENT

420
00:24:44,483 --> 00:24:47,569
"Dear Ms. Hong Gil-sun,
I hope you're well."

421
00:24:47,652 --> 00:24:51,448
"I'm Lee Yang-buk, Joil Textile Factory
president's secretary."

422
00:24:52,365 --> 00:24:53,533
Joil Textile Factory?

423
00:24:54,159 --> 00:24:55,785
Oh, that Joil Textile Factory.

424
00:24:56,453 --> 00:25:00,624
"We extend our sincere thanks to you

425
00:25:00,707 --> 00:25:04,669
for applying to serve
as our young lady's study companion

426
00:25:04,753 --> 00:25:06,963
during her studies in America."

427
00:25:08,048 --> 00:25:08,965
America?

428
00:25:09,508 --> 00:25:13,345
"There will be an interview
at our president's house

429
00:25:13,428 --> 00:25:18,767
at 1:00 p.m. on Saturday, April 9th."

430
00:25:18,850 --> 00:25:23,730
"If selected, we'll cover all tuition
and living expenses

431
00:25:23,813 --> 00:25:25,440
for two years of study in America."

432
00:25:26,274 --> 00:25:27,150
What?

433
00:25:28,276 --> 00:25:29,152
Hang on.

434
00:25:29,903 --> 00:25:32,405
"A study companion
during her studies in America."

435
00:25:35,534 --> 00:25:37,369
Oh my goodness.

436
00:25:39,120 --> 00:25:40,247
What's the date today?

437
00:25:41,540 --> 00:25:42,457
April 8th.

438
00:25:42,958 --> 00:25:44,125
The interview is tomorrow.

439
00:25:46,836 --> 00:25:48,421
I can't believe this.

440
00:25:48,505 --> 00:25:50,215
Hey, No-ma!

441
00:25:50,298 --> 00:25:51,174
No-ma!

442
00:25:51,258 --> 00:25:52,884
- One moment.
- What's going on?

443
00:25:52,968 --> 00:25:54,511
Kkot-nim, we'll be right back.

444
00:25:54,594 --> 00:25:55,554
Be right back.

445
00:25:55,637 --> 00:25:56,680
Where are you going?

446
00:25:56,763 --> 00:25:57,764
It won't take long.

447
00:26:06,356 --> 00:26:08,733
It's hard to tell,
but have you seen anyone like this?

448
00:26:08,817 --> 00:26:10,652
- Nope.
- Bad drawing, isn't it?

449
00:26:10,735 --> 00:26:12,404
Someone must've drawn it blindfolded.

450
00:26:14,990 --> 00:26:16,658
Have you had a female customer like this?

451
00:26:16,741 --> 00:26:18,451
- She wears glasses.
- No idea.

452
00:26:18,535 --> 00:26:20,161
How could anyone tell from that?

453
00:26:20,245 --> 00:26:22,372
- Look carefully.
- Go away.

454
00:26:22,455 --> 00:26:23,373
Wait…

455
00:26:23,873 --> 00:26:25,750
Mister.

456
00:26:25,834 --> 00:26:29,004
Look, have you ever had a female customer
who looks like this?

457
00:26:29,087 --> 00:26:30,839
She's from Busan and has an accent.

458
00:26:33,008 --> 00:26:33,842
Glasses.

459
00:26:33,925 --> 00:26:35,510
Yes, exactly. Glasses.

460
00:26:35,594 --> 00:26:37,470
Glasses, right?

461
00:26:39,139 --> 00:26:40,098
Do I know you?

462
00:26:42,350 --> 00:26:44,102
Are you Ms. Hong Gil-sun?

463
00:26:44,185 --> 00:26:46,271
Yes, but who are you?

464
00:26:46,354 --> 00:26:49,608
I was sent on an errand
for the president of Joil Textile Factory.

465
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:52,694
The Joil Textile Factory president?

466
00:26:52,777 --> 00:26:53,862
What brings you here?

467
00:26:54,738 --> 00:26:59,200
He sent me to inform you
that today's interview has been canceled.

468
00:26:59,951 --> 00:27:02,454
What? Canceled?

469
00:27:02,537 --> 00:27:03,371
Why?

470
00:27:03,455 --> 00:27:06,082
He's decided to go with another candidate.

471
00:27:06,708 --> 00:27:09,169
He said he was deeply sorry
for the inconvenience

472
00:27:09,252 --> 00:27:11,046
and asked me to give you this.

473
00:27:11,129 --> 00:27:12,422
What is it?

474
00:27:13,006 --> 00:27:15,050
You came all the way
from Busan for nothing.

475
00:27:15,133 --> 00:27:18,219
He told me to give you this
to cover your train fare

476
00:27:18,303 --> 00:27:20,138
as a token of his apology.

477
00:27:24,225 --> 00:27:25,101
<i>Twenty won.</i>

478
00:27:25,185 --> 00:27:27,145
<i>That's a department store clerk's</i>
<i>monthly salary.</i>

479
00:27:27,228 --> 00:27:28,438
I'm so sorry.

480
00:27:28,521 --> 00:27:29,648
<i>It's no small amount.</i>

481
00:27:30,523 --> 00:27:31,900
<i>She understands that</i>

482
00:27:31,983 --> 00:27:35,278
<i>sometimes she has to give something first</i>
<i>to get what she wants.</i>

483
00:27:41,034 --> 00:27:42,035
Oh my.

484
00:27:45,080 --> 00:27:47,457
Please wait here for a moment, Ms. Hong.

485
00:27:48,249 --> 00:27:49,250
Sure.

486
00:27:49,876 --> 00:27:50,794
Thanks.

487
00:27:52,962 --> 00:27:54,005
Young lady?

488
00:27:55,882 --> 00:27:57,634
She looks so gorgeous.

489
00:27:58,510 --> 00:28:01,930
She looks like actress Kim Yeon-sil,
doesn't she?

490
00:28:03,723 --> 00:28:06,810
No, she's prettier than Kim Yeon-sil.

491
00:28:06,893 --> 00:28:08,103
Right?

492
00:28:08,728 --> 00:28:10,230
Then I'll go bring her.

493
00:28:11,231 --> 00:28:12,232
Okay.

494
00:28:15,527 --> 00:28:17,278
What's his problem? She's pretty.

495
00:28:48,226 --> 00:28:50,520
I should make a good impression,
so I'll just look.

496
00:28:51,187 --> 00:28:53,106
Don't be penny-wise and pound-foolish.

497
00:29:01,614 --> 00:29:04,325
Hello, I'm Hong Gil-sun…

498
00:29:18,256 --> 00:29:19,507
So you're saying

499
00:29:20,049 --> 00:29:24,345
you set the whole thing up,
and I played right into your hands?

500
00:29:25,180 --> 00:29:27,182
If that's how you want to put it,

501
00:29:27,265 --> 00:29:28,224
then yes.

502
00:29:31,269 --> 00:29:33,813
You said your business fell through
because of me last time.

503
00:29:33,897 --> 00:29:34,898
We're even now, right?

504
00:29:36,316 --> 00:29:38,651
I hope we never see each other again.

505
00:29:40,236 --> 00:29:43,114
What about my 20 won?
Did I just lose that?

506
00:29:48,953 --> 00:29:49,996
I'll send you to America.

507
00:29:51,748 --> 00:29:52,582
What?

508
00:29:53,166 --> 00:29:55,043
You said you wanted to go to America.

509
00:29:55,919 --> 00:29:57,212
In return, work with us.

510
00:29:58,505 --> 00:30:00,089
Work at the Government-General.

511
00:30:01,925 --> 00:30:03,092
The Government-General?

512
00:30:05,804 --> 00:30:06,888
You can't be serious.

513
00:30:07,472 --> 00:30:08,973
Are you saying

514
00:30:09,808 --> 00:30:11,518
you want me to be a spy?

515
00:30:11,601 --> 00:30:12,602
Yes.

516
00:30:13,311 --> 00:30:16,231
Infiltrate the Government-General
as a spy and work for us.

517
00:30:17,315 --> 00:30:21,152
The Japanese keep infiltrating
our government and independence groups.

518
00:30:21,236 --> 00:30:23,947
The Japanese aren't the only ones
who can plant spies.

519
00:30:25,073 --> 00:30:27,575
Look, I'm not an idiot.

520
00:30:27,659 --> 00:30:29,994
I ended up falling
for the promise of America today,

521
00:30:30,078 --> 00:30:33,122
but I'm usually the one who tricks others,
not the other way around.

522
00:30:33,206 --> 00:30:35,917
You enjoyed messing with me today,
so why keep pushing?

523
00:30:37,377 --> 00:30:40,588
Even for a thousand won, I'm not doing it.

524
00:30:40,672 --> 00:30:43,174
I don't want to get involved
in the independence movement.

525
00:30:43,258 --> 00:30:45,218
I don't want to die
before I make it to America.

526
00:30:45,301 --> 00:30:48,805
I believe I told you the other day.
You can't go to America now.

527
00:30:48,888 --> 00:30:51,558
You keep telling me I can't go there.

528
00:30:51,641 --> 00:30:54,060
Is it fun to mess
with someone's desperate hopes?

529
00:30:54,143 --> 00:30:56,688
I'm talking
about the current situation in America.

530
00:30:57,605 --> 00:31:00,316
For years now,
America has banned immigration from Asia

531
00:31:00,400 --> 00:31:01,734
based on the Immigration Act.

532
00:31:01,818 --> 00:31:03,236
Don't be ridiculous.

533
00:31:03,319 --> 00:31:04,779
I've never heard of such a thing.

534
00:31:06,072 --> 00:31:08,867
Just because you don't know
doesn't mean it doesn't exist.

535
00:31:08,950 --> 00:31:12,161
But we'll send you to America

536
00:31:12,662 --> 00:31:14,455
as long as you accept our offer.

537
00:31:14,539 --> 00:31:15,748
Excuse me.

538
00:31:15,832 --> 00:31:18,126
You're contradicting yourself.

539
00:31:18,209 --> 00:31:21,796
You just said American law
won't allow me to go to America.

540
00:31:21,880 --> 00:31:25,174
Then how exactly
are you going to get me there?

541
00:31:25,258 --> 00:31:26,467
Did you forget?

542
00:31:27,343 --> 00:31:30,054
My husband is American,
and he's a journalist.

543
00:31:30,138 --> 00:31:32,056
It means he's not an ordinary Joseon man.

544
00:31:32,640 --> 00:31:34,601
Whatever. I'm out of here.

545
00:31:36,436 --> 00:31:39,647
Don't ever think about trying to hurt me.

546
00:31:39,731 --> 00:31:41,149
If I'm not home by tonight,

547
00:31:41,232 --> 00:31:45,153
they'll take the bullets and the suitcase
to Jongno Police Station and report them.

548
00:31:45,236 --> 00:31:48,197
Do you think the police will believe you?

549
00:31:48,281 --> 00:31:50,283
You're with the Gugukdan, aren't you?

550
00:31:51,284 --> 00:31:52,911
You'd better let me go quietly--

551
00:31:52,994 --> 00:31:53,995
Lee Ga-kyung.

552
00:31:56,539 --> 00:31:58,708
Who is that?

553
00:32:00,209 --> 00:32:01,085
Lee Ga-kyung?

554
00:32:01,169 --> 00:32:04,589
As an orphan, you worked as a live-in maid
for an American missionary.

555
00:32:04,672 --> 00:32:08,092
You've been living in the shantytown
in Gyodang-ri these past few years.

556
00:32:08,968 --> 00:32:10,678
You live with a young rickshaw puller

557
00:32:10,762 --> 00:32:12,722
and a little girl who can't walk.

558
00:32:12,805 --> 00:32:15,266
They're not your husband and daughter.

559
00:32:15,350 --> 00:32:16,851
Lee No-ma and Bang Kkot-nim?

560
00:32:17,727 --> 00:32:19,395
I don't know what you're talking about.

561
00:32:19,479 --> 00:32:22,065
You told those two to report it,
didn't you?

562
00:32:22,148 --> 00:32:25,109
But who do you think
those two are with now?

563
00:32:25,193 --> 00:32:28,655
I really have no idea
what you're talking about.

564
00:32:29,364 --> 00:32:30,406
Fine.

565
00:32:32,700 --> 00:32:33,993
Let me ask you something.

566
00:32:34,077 --> 00:32:37,080
You're smart, resourceful,
and even fluent in English.

567
00:32:37,163 --> 00:32:38,498
Why do you live like this?

568
00:32:38,581 --> 00:32:40,792
That's none of your business.

569
00:32:41,626 --> 00:32:44,128
Then why do you live like this?

570
00:32:44,212 --> 00:32:46,381
Why do you fight for independence?

571
00:32:46,464 --> 00:32:48,049
You could die doing that.

572
00:32:48,132 --> 00:32:50,426
I might as well have died a long time ago.

573
00:32:50,510 --> 00:32:51,886
So I'm not afraid of death.

574
00:32:56,516 --> 00:32:58,559
Someday, I hope you'll make it

575
00:32:59,268 --> 00:33:01,479
to America, Ga-kyung.

576
00:33:02,814 --> 00:33:03,940
Go there

577
00:33:04,524 --> 00:33:06,067
and be happy.

578
00:33:07,735 --> 00:33:09,988
I told you, I'm not Lee Ga-kyung.

579
00:33:19,664 --> 00:33:20,581
But you know what?

580
00:33:21,833 --> 00:33:25,128
If you plan to die fighting
for independence, don't have children.

581
00:33:26,129 --> 00:33:27,422
Whatever the reason,

582
00:33:27,505 --> 00:33:30,133
there's nothing worse
than leaving a child all alone.

583
00:33:47,191 --> 00:33:51,195
Why did you just let her go
when she rejected the offer?

584
00:34:01,247 --> 00:34:02,665
That's what I'm wondering.

585
00:34:03,750 --> 00:34:05,626
Who says I can't go to America?

586
00:34:06,210 --> 00:34:07,086
Unbelievable.

587
00:34:08,087 --> 00:34:11,174
I'll go to America just fine on my own.
Spare me your concern!

588
00:34:23,019 --> 00:34:26,022
I really can't go to America
even if I save up enough money?

589
00:34:27,148 --> 00:34:27,982
Why not?

590
00:34:29,984 --> 00:34:30,902
Damn it.

591
00:34:32,236 --> 00:34:33,905
She wasn't lying.

592
00:34:37,450 --> 00:34:38,785
Lee No-ma, Bang Kkot…

593
00:34:39,285 --> 00:34:40,328
Kkot-nim!

594
00:34:40,411 --> 00:34:41,954
Lee No-ma!

595
00:34:42,038 --> 00:34:43,122
Bang Kkot-nim!

596
00:34:49,921 --> 00:34:50,755
Thank goodness.

597
00:34:51,339 --> 00:34:52,340
They're all right.

598
00:34:56,803 --> 00:34:59,514
What the hell? And now this one's a lie?

599
00:35:01,891 --> 00:35:02,809
I'm exhausted.

600
00:35:03,518 --> 00:35:04,352
Kkot-nim.

601
00:35:08,439 --> 00:35:09,273
Bang Kkot-nim.

602
00:35:10,691 --> 00:35:13,528
Why are you crying?

603
00:35:14,654 --> 00:35:16,114
Why are you crying, Kkot-nim?

604
00:35:16,781 --> 00:35:18,032
Did the kids pick on you again?

605
00:35:19,325 --> 00:35:20,159
Hey.

606
00:35:21,035 --> 00:35:21,869
You…

607
00:35:23,121 --> 00:35:24,497
What happened to your face?

608
00:35:25,665 --> 00:35:26,499
Why…

609
00:35:35,174 --> 00:35:36,008
You're here?

610
00:35:45,518 --> 00:35:46,853
What are you doing?

611
00:35:47,770 --> 00:35:48,938
What do you think?

612
00:35:49,021 --> 00:35:51,399
I'm checking out the little stash

613
00:35:51,482 --> 00:35:56,028
my pretty Ga-kyung
has been hiding under my roof.

614
00:35:57,655 --> 00:35:59,448
Wow, how much is all this?

615
00:35:59,532 --> 00:36:02,910
One won, five won… This is impressive.

616
00:36:02,994 --> 00:36:04,745
It's not a secret stash.
It's my daily wages.

617
00:36:04,829 --> 00:36:05,997
Give it back.

618
00:36:06,622 --> 00:36:07,665
Daily wages?

619
00:36:08,291 --> 00:36:10,126
You pretty little bitch.

620
00:36:10,668 --> 00:36:13,796
You're quite the liar, aren't you?

621
00:36:14,463 --> 00:36:16,382
Don't take that. It's my money.

622
00:36:16,465 --> 00:36:17,842
Give back my money!

623
00:36:19,510 --> 00:36:20,511
Your money?

624
00:36:21,220 --> 00:36:22,054
Your money?

625
00:36:23,472 --> 00:36:25,016
What money here is yours?

626
00:36:25,099 --> 00:36:29,020
Who do you think lets you make a living
picking other people's pockets?

627
00:36:29,687 --> 00:36:30,563
Ga-kyung!

628
00:36:30,646 --> 00:36:33,149
And you still owe me, remember?

629
00:36:33,232 --> 00:36:34,609
Don't hit her. Just talk to her.

630
00:36:34,692 --> 00:36:36,944
- Let go already!
- She really saved it from her wages.

631
00:36:37,528 --> 00:36:38,529
Get the hell off me!

632
00:36:40,948 --> 00:36:41,824
Answer me.

633
00:36:42,450 --> 00:36:43,367
I said answer me.

634
00:36:43,451 --> 00:36:46,537
Whose turf is Gyeongseong Station?

635
00:36:46,621 --> 00:36:48,581
Who's the one bribing the police

636
00:36:48,664 --> 00:36:51,459
to look the other way when you pickpocket?

637
00:36:51,542 --> 00:36:53,211
Whose house do you think this is?

638
00:36:53,294 --> 00:36:55,421
If I hadn't taken you in back then,

639
00:36:55,504 --> 00:36:58,341
that Sinjeong Red-Light District
would be your home now.

640
00:36:58,966 --> 00:36:59,800
Do you know that?

641
00:37:00,551 --> 00:37:01,677
After all I've done for you,

642
00:37:01,761 --> 00:37:04,013
you stole my money
and tried to sneak out of the country.

643
00:37:04,722 --> 00:37:08,017
I let you off once, and now
you're stashing money behind my back?

644
00:37:08,601 --> 00:37:10,478
That's enough, Boss. Please stop.

645
00:37:13,272 --> 00:37:14,982
You're in on this together, aren't you?

646
00:37:15,650 --> 00:37:17,944
When you asked me
to let this bitch stay here,

647
00:37:18,027 --> 00:37:18,903
what did I say?

648
00:37:18,986 --> 00:37:21,781
I warned you not to stab me in the back
over some bitch.

649
00:37:21,864 --> 00:37:23,491
Did I or didn't I?

650
00:37:25,076 --> 00:37:26,244
It's not like that!

651
00:37:26,327 --> 00:37:27,453
Don't hit him!

652
00:37:34,627 --> 00:37:35,836
You damn bitch!

653
00:37:37,838 --> 00:37:41,050
You said if I caught you
hiding money from me,

654
00:37:41,133 --> 00:37:43,135
you'd pay me back with your body.

655
00:37:44,971 --> 00:37:46,555
Today is the day.

656
00:37:48,724 --> 00:37:49,558
Get over here.

657
00:37:51,519 --> 00:37:52,436
Come here.

658
00:37:54,814 --> 00:37:55,982
Hold still.

659
00:38:00,653 --> 00:38:03,197
Stop, Boss. I'll give you all the money.

660
00:38:03,281 --> 00:38:05,157
Leave Ga-kyung alone.

661
00:38:07,743 --> 00:38:09,036
For goodness' sake!

662
00:38:09,120 --> 00:38:10,621
Do you really want to die? Damn it.

663
00:38:10,705 --> 00:38:11,789
Back off.

664
00:38:12,832 --> 00:38:13,958
Don't.

665
00:38:14,041 --> 00:38:15,209
Stop, Boss.

666
00:38:15,293 --> 00:38:17,003
- Let go.
- Boss.

667
00:38:17,795 --> 00:38:20,089
I said let go! Let go!

668
00:38:22,466 --> 00:38:23,968
You bastard.

669
00:38:24,051 --> 00:38:25,886
Sorry. Please stop.

670
00:38:27,179 --> 00:38:29,140
- Let go!
- Boss.

671
00:38:29,223 --> 00:38:31,309
- I said just let go of me!
- Boss.

672
00:38:34,520 --> 00:38:37,231
I'm sorry, please forgive me.

673
00:38:40,109 --> 00:38:41,652
Get off me!

674
00:38:46,240 --> 00:38:48,451
Lee No-ma, you idiot.

675
00:38:48,534 --> 00:38:49,994
Snap out of it.

676
00:38:50,077 --> 00:38:52,163
Do you know what kind of bitch she is?

677
00:38:52,246 --> 00:38:54,415
Apparently, her mom

678
00:38:54,915 --> 00:38:56,834
went screwing around with a Jap.

679
00:38:58,210 --> 00:38:59,587
She was a <i>gisaeng</i>

680
00:39:00,546 --> 00:39:02,006
who shacked up with a Jap.

681
00:39:05,551 --> 00:39:08,012
When your mom and dad

682
00:39:08,095 --> 00:39:12,183
were getting shot dead in the head
shouting, "Independence for Korea,"

683
00:39:12,266 --> 00:39:15,186
her mom was sleeping with a Jap.

684
00:39:15,269 --> 00:39:16,729
You didn't know, did you?

685
00:39:17,938 --> 00:39:18,773
No.

686
00:39:19,732 --> 00:39:20,900
That's not true, right?

687
00:39:21,442 --> 00:39:25,529
Ga-kyung's mother died of tuberculosis
when she was little.

688
00:39:25,613 --> 00:39:26,655
Tuberculosis?

689
00:39:30,493 --> 00:39:31,744
Tuberculosis.

690
00:39:34,163 --> 00:39:35,748
Lee Ga-kyung, seriously,

691
00:39:35,831 --> 00:39:39,877
you're one hell of a liar.
I have to hand it to you.

692
00:39:40,544 --> 00:39:43,923
How could you hide that
from No-ma, of all people?

693
00:39:44,006 --> 00:39:45,925
Were you afraid

694
00:39:46,008 --> 00:39:47,968
he would throw you out if he found out?

695
00:39:48,761 --> 00:39:49,595
Hey.

696
00:39:50,179 --> 00:39:51,472
Do you know why

697
00:39:51,555 --> 00:39:53,474
that bitch's so good at pickpocketing?

698
00:39:55,017 --> 00:39:56,685
It's in her blood.

699
00:39:57,269 --> 00:39:59,939
She's got the blood of the Japanese
who stole our country.

700
00:40:00,022 --> 00:40:03,067
The blood of a thief is bound to show.

701
00:40:04,235 --> 00:40:07,530
Hey, do you think your mother
hooked up with a Jap just once?

702
00:40:08,114 --> 00:40:11,450
You know deep down
you've probably got Jap blood in you,

703
00:40:12,201 --> 00:40:13,160
don't you?

704
00:40:16,455 --> 00:40:17,540
You pitiful wench.

705
00:40:19,834 --> 00:40:21,252
Watch your mouth.

706
00:40:21,335 --> 00:40:22,253
What?

707
00:40:22,336 --> 00:40:24,088
She's still your mother,

708
00:40:24,171 --> 00:40:25,798
so you're upset because I insulted her?

709
00:40:25,881 --> 00:40:27,049
No!

710
00:40:28,843 --> 00:40:30,928
Watch your mouth about me.

711
00:40:31,470 --> 00:40:32,304
What?

712
00:40:33,848 --> 00:40:34,682
I am…

713
00:40:36,475 --> 00:40:38,060
not pitiful.

714
00:40:39,311 --> 00:40:40,271
What are you, then?

715
00:40:42,690 --> 00:40:44,817
If you're not pitiful,

716
00:40:45,985 --> 00:40:47,194
then what are you?

717
00:41:04,128 --> 00:41:06,088
- Did you see her face?
- I did.

718
00:41:10,092 --> 00:41:11,427
<i>You disgust me.</i>

719
00:41:11,510 --> 00:41:12,845
That filthy blood!

720
00:41:12,928 --> 00:41:14,346
Get out of my house!

721
00:41:27,193 --> 00:41:30,905
Why are you hitting it so hard?
Are you mad at your husband?

722
00:41:31,405 --> 00:41:33,073
Oh, I am.

723
00:41:33,866 --> 00:41:36,202
I haven't seen the widow
from the shop lately.

724
00:41:36,285 --> 00:41:39,205
She had an affair with a Japanese man
and ran off to Japan.

725
00:41:39,288 --> 00:41:40,998
No wonder I haven't seen her around.

726
00:41:45,127 --> 00:41:46,754
Hey, wait.

727
00:41:47,338 --> 00:41:49,965
Aren't you the live-in maid
at the American's house?

728
00:41:51,884 --> 00:41:53,594
It must be hard.

729
00:41:54,220 --> 00:41:55,846
Why don't you work at a textile factory?

730
00:41:55,930 --> 00:41:58,599
The factory is nice and warm.

731
00:42:01,560 --> 00:42:02,895
Don't come any closer!

732
00:42:04,688 --> 00:42:06,023
Why do you keep saying no?

733
00:42:06,690 --> 00:42:09,568
Weren't you after a Japanese guy too?

734
00:42:09,652 --> 00:42:11,195
Just like your mother?

735
00:42:47,273 --> 00:42:48,357
I'll do it.

736
00:42:50,276 --> 00:42:52,861
Send me to America, and I'll work for you

737
00:42:53,487 --> 00:42:54,321
as your spy.

738
00:43:21,849 --> 00:43:23,309
<i>There are conditions, though.</i>

739
00:43:23,976 --> 00:43:26,186
I want a bonus on top of my base pay.

740
00:43:26,895 --> 00:43:28,063
A bonus?

741
00:43:28,147 --> 00:43:31,233
I hear Government-General employees
get a bonus on top of their base salary.

742
00:43:31,317 --> 00:43:33,110
That's only for Japanese employees.

743
00:43:33,193 --> 00:43:34,862
You're a Joseon employee,
so you don't get a bonus.

744
00:43:34,945 --> 00:43:35,779
Is that so?

745
00:43:36,488 --> 00:43:39,950
Then the Gugukdan can pay me the bonus.

746
00:43:40,034 --> 00:43:41,368
That's my first condition.

747
00:43:43,329 --> 00:43:44,455
What's the second one?

748
00:43:45,706 --> 00:43:48,083
Let me eat and sleep here for free.

749
00:43:50,586 --> 00:43:52,796
I admit, I'm after money.

750
00:43:52,880 --> 00:43:54,089
But living under the same roof

751
00:43:54,173 --> 00:43:56,175
won't raise suspicion
even if we're together.

752
00:43:56,759 --> 00:43:59,094
I'll stay here as a tenant,
not a live-in maid.

753
00:44:00,095 --> 00:44:02,014
I'll only do this

754
00:44:02,723 --> 00:44:04,183
for three months.

755
00:44:57,820 --> 00:45:00,531
<i>After three months, send me to America.</i>

756
00:45:01,281 --> 00:45:03,367
We have conditions too.

757
00:45:04,243 --> 00:45:05,577
First, don't ask any questions.

758
00:45:06,662 --> 00:45:09,123
<i>You just do what we tell you.</i>

759
00:45:10,332 --> 00:45:11,875
<i>You don't get to ask why.</i>

760
00:45:12,626 --> 00:45:13,460
<i>Second,</i>

761
00:45:14,253 --> 00:45:16,171
<i>don't steal anything from this house.</i>

762
00:45:17,423 --> 00:45:18,549
<i>And finally,</i>

763
00:45:19,883 --> 00:45:21,301
don't betray us.

764
00:45:22,469 --> 00:45:24,263
There's no mercy for traitors.

765
00:45:24,346 --> 00:45:26,348
I was about to say the same thing.

766
00:45:27,933 --> 00:45:31,687
Hideo isn't Sato's real son.
Sato adopted a Joseon boy.

767
00:45:32,813 --> 00:45:33,856
He's from Joseon?

768
00:45:33,939 --> 00:45:34,815
That's right.

769
00:45:34,898 --> 00:45:37,526
<i>He was an orphan</i>
<i>living on the streets in Gyeongseong.</i>

770
00:45:37,609 --> 00:45:40,237
<i>He was adopted by Sato</i>
<i>when he was about 12.</i>

771
00:45:42,030 --> 00:45:44,199
The inspector general comes

772
00:45:44,283 --> 00:45:47,327
<i>from a fallen samurai family</i>
<i>that came to Joseon in his father's time.</i>

773
00:45:47,411 --> 00:45:49,455
To overcome being a colonial resident,

774
00:45:49,538 --> 00:45:52,374
he attended
the Imperial Japanese Army Academy

775
00:45:52,458 --> 00:45:55,210
<i>and returned to Joseon as a soldier.</i>

776
00:45:56,462 --> 00:45:58,714
He adopted a Joseon orphan

777
00:45:58,797 --> 00:46:01,884
to show how faithfully
he supported Japan's policies.

778
00:46:05,637 --> 00:46:07,931
His adopted son, Sato Hideo,

779
00:46:08,015 --> 00:46:09,433
<i>is a Joseon man only by birth.</i>

780
00:46:09,516 --> 00:46:11,769
<i>He also graduated from</i>
<i>the Imperial Japanese Army Academy</i>

781
00:46:11,852 --> 00:46:13,854
and works
for the Government-General of Joseon.

782
00:46:14,938 --> 00:46:17,900
He's Japanese through and through.

783
00:46:17,983 --> 00:46:20,944
Most importantly,
he's the inspector general's son.

784
00:46:22,237 --> 00:46:23,822
Keep your guard up around him.

785
00:46:24,698 --> 00:46:25,532
<i>Okay.</i>

786
00:46:26,450 --> 00:46:27,618
<i>I'll keep that in mind.</i>

787
00:46:28,368 --> 00:46:31,079
INTERPRETER SATO HIDEO
INTERPRETER TRAINEE KEUM SEO-HEE

788
00:46:33,373 --> 00:46:34,458
<i>Good morning.</i>

789
00:46:36,084 --> 00:46:40,798
I'm Keum Seo-hee,
the new interpreter trainee assigned here.

790
00:46:40,881 --> 00:46:43,425
I look forward to working with you.

791
00:46:43,926 --> 00:46:46,512
Likewise. I look forward to it.

792
00:46:48,096 --> 00:46:50,182
I'll do my best to assist you.

793
00:46:51,391 --> 00:46:52,601
Thank you.

794
00:46:56,271 --> 00:46:59,107
<i>The League of Nations Commission</i>
<i>will come to Gyeongseong soon.</i>

795
00:47:01,151 --> 00:47:03,904
The Manchurian Incident,
the Shanghai Incident.

796
00:47:04,863 --> 00:47:07,991
China claims
Japan illegally invaded Manchuria.

797
00:47:08,075 --> 00:47:12,037
Japan claims it was exercising
its right to self-defense.

798
00:47:13,497 --> 00:47:16,041
That's why
the League of Nations Commission

799
00:47:16,124 --> 00:47:19,336
is going around Manchuria
conducting their investigation.

800
00:47:19,419 --> 00:47:22,005
They're getting paid
to be there, after all.

801
00:47:23,882 --> 00:47:26,051
But they're coming here as well.

802
00:47:26,134 --> 00:47:27,094
Isn't it obvious?

803
00:47:27,177 --> 00:47:29,137
To get a glimpse of Manchuria's future,

804
00:47:29,221 --> 00:47:32,391
they can look at Joseon
under Japanese rule.

805
00:47:32,474 --> 00:47:34,726
We received word that the Commission

806
00:47:34,810 --> 00:47:37,479
wants to visit our newspaper
while they're in Gyeongseong.

807
00:47:37,980 --> 00:47:38,814
Is that so?

808
00:47:39,398 --> 00:47:41,692
Yes, and as you know, sir,

809
00:47:41,775 --> 00:47:44,444
one of the two Western journalists
coming with the Commission

810
00:47:44,528 --> 00:47:46,405
is our paper's bureau chief
in the Far East.

811
00:47:47,990 --> 00:47:48,824
Right.

812
00:47:50,742 --> 00:47:54,454
And wouldn't you know it,
I'm Joseon-American.

813
00:47:55,747 --> 00:47:57,875
If I were to tell the Commission

814
00:47:57,958 --> 00:48:00,669
that Japan is doing a fine job
developing Joseon,

815
00:48:01,962 --> 00:48:03,463
that'd make a useful reference

816
00:48:03,547 --> 00:48:05,591
for their report, don't you think?

817
00:48:05,674 --> 00:48:08,260
If all goes well, the League of Nations

818
00:48:08,343 --> 00:48:11,263
might even recognize Manchukuo
as a legitimate independent state.

819
00:48:13,307 --> 00:48:15,434
I'll leave it in your hands.

820
00:48:17,519 --> 00:48:19,438
Of course. I'll do everything I can.

821
00:48:20,606 --> 00:48:22,024
Speaking of which,

822
00:48:22,107 --> 00:48:25,694
while they're in Gyeongseong,
they'll need interpreters, I'd imagine.

823
00:48:26,612 --> 00:48:27,446
Indeed.

824
00:48:29,406 --> 00:48:32,075
Yes, the Government-General's
Foreign Affairs Division

825
00:48:32,159 --> 00:48:34,494
will provide interpretation
during their visit.

826
00:48:35,412 --> 00:48:36,788
Just as I thought.

827
00:48:41,293 --> 00:48:44,004
Do you happen to remember
our live-in maid?

828
00:48:45,589 --> 00:48:46,924
The maid…

829
00:48:48,884 --> 00:48:49,718
I remember her.

830
00:48:49,801 --> 00:48:51,386
So you remember her.

831
00:48:53,138 --> 00:48:54,681
Her English is quite good.

832
00:48:55,933 --> 00:48:59,019
She's very bright.
She can handle the job with no problem.

833
00:48:59,102 --> 00:49:02,773
It would be wonderful if she could work
at the Government-General

834
00:49:02,856 --> 00:49:06,944
and assist with the Commission's visit
alongside me.

835
00:49:07,736 --> 00:49:10,197
Wouldn't that benefit us both?

836
00:49:14,451 --> 00:49:15,285
I was wondering…

837
00:49:25,128 --> 00:49:26,964
That's right. She's my girl.

838
00:49:29,091 --> 00:49:31,051
That explains it.

839
00:49:48,819 --> 00:49:51,154
First a pickpocket, then a live-in maid,

840
00:49:52,155 --> 00:49:54,074
now a Government-General's
interpreter trainee.

841
00:49:55,409 --> 00:49:56,702
What is all this about?

842
00:49:56,785 --> 00:49:58,537
I'm trying to make a living.

843
00:49:58,620 --> 00:50:00,831
The Governor-General job pays very well.

844
00:50:20,892 --> 00:50:22,477
Yeah, I figured.

845
00:50:22,561 --> 00:50:26,314
So you know a few words of English,
and you want to play the career woman.

846
00:50:57,345 --> 00:50:58,930
Need I say more?

847
00:51:00,515 --> 00:51:02,642
I assumed you'd know that much.

848
00:51:04,061 --> 00:51:08,315
Who would keep their lover
in the same office, right next to them?

849
00:51:08,398 --> 00:51:09,649
They'd get caught in no time.

850
00:51:14,154 --> 00:51:17,657
Ms. Keum, have you ever told anything
that was actually true?

851
00:51:18,241 --> 00:51:19,076
What?

852
00:51:19,159 --> 00:51:22,037
I'm asking if you've ever been honest,
even just once.

853
00:51:22,996 --> 00:51:24,539
Of course I have.

854
00:51:24,623 --> 00:51:26,666
Do you think I lie all the time?

855
00:51:26,750 --> 00:51:28,043
Isn't that another lie?

856
00:51:29,544 --> 00:51:32,214
You really don't trust people, do you?

857
00:51:32,297 --> 00:51:33,715
Can you blame me?

858
00:51:34,591 --> 00:51:37,094
Think about everything you've done to me.

859
00:51:39,638 --> 00:51:40,472
Ms. Keum.

860
00:51:41,765 --> 00:51:43,975
This is the fourth time we have met.

861
00:51:44,059 --> 00:51:45,644
<i>First at Gyeongseong Station,</i>

862
00:51:45,727 --> 00:51:48,063
<i>then at Mr. Yoo Philip's house.</i>

863
00:51:48,146 --> 00:51:50,649
<i>The third was</i>
<i>at The First Jongno Pawnshop.</i>

864
00:51:50,732 --> 00:51:52,567
<i>Let's chalk those up to coincidence.</i>

865
00:51:52,651 --> 00:51:54,444
But the fourth time
can't be a coincidence.

866
00:51:56,488 --> 00:51:57,739
You asked Mr. Yoo

867
00:51:57,823 --> 00:52:00,534
to join the Government-General,
in my division, of all places.

868
00:52:03,286 --> 00:52:04,246
I give you my word.

869
00:52:05,038 --> 00:52:08,792
If you're honest with me just once,
without deceiving me,

870
00:52:10,001 --> 00:52:11,586
I'll consider you my colleague.

871
00:52:12,129 --> 00:52:16,424
Why did you go through Mr. Yoo
to come to the Government-General?

872
00:52:20,929 --> 00:52:22,764
Why do you keep getting involved with me?

873
00:52:26,977 --> 00:52:28,353
I knew you'd have nothing to say.

874
00:52:29,146 --> 00:52:30,021
I like you.

875
00:52:33,650 --> 00:52:36,236
I've had feelings for you
since we first met.

876
00:52:39,489 --> 00:52:41,950
That's why I came here.

877
00:52:55,422 --> 00:52:58,550
Now, will you consider me your colleague?

878
00:53:01,178 --> 00:53:02,512
You're surprisingly easy to fool.

879
00:53:03,305 --> 00:53:04,347
Sorry?

880
00:53:05,348 --> 00:53:06,474
That was a lie.

881
00:53:07,309 --> 00:53:09,936
You're not the only one who can lie.

882
00:53:12,189 --> 00:53:13,982
You're a really good liar.

883
00:53:14,065 --> 00:53:17,110
I almost fell for it back then too.

884
00:53:17,903 --> 00:53:18,820
The princess?

885
00:53:19,487 --> 00:53:21,323
The day you stole my wallet?

886
00:53:31,499 --> 00:53:35,170
If you lay a hand on me,
you can forget about finding it.

887
00:53:39,925 --> 00:53:43,094
Wait, did you think
I was going to hit you?

888
00:53:45,055 --> 00:53:47,224
What kind of person do you take me for?

889
00:53:47,307 --> 00:53:48,600
Give me the pawn ticket.

890
00:53:48,683 --> 00:53:49,893
Otherwise,

891
00:53:50,477 --> 00:53:52,103
I'll tell Mr. Yoo all about this.

892
00:53:54,105 --> 00:53:55,273
About what?

893
00:53:55,357 --> 00:53:57,567
What you just said to me.

894
00:54:00,904 --> 00:54:03,198
What did I just say?

895
00:54:04,866 --> 00:54:06,117
Don't you remember?

896
00:54:06,201 --> 00:54:08,620
Do you go around
saying such things to anyone?

897
00:54:08,703 --> 00:54:11,623
I don't say that to just anybody.
I said it to you.

898
00:54:12,540 --> 00:54:13,792
For the first time in my life.

899
00:54:17,379 --> 00:54:21,007
You said if I was honest with you,
you'd consider me your colleague.

900
00:54:21,091 --> 00:54:22,259
That's why I said it.

901
00:54:24,719 --> 00:54:27,222
You said you'd tell Mr. Yoo what I said.

902
00:54:32,060 --> 00:54:35,855
It looks like you really believed me.

903
00:54:48,910 --> 00:54:50,578
Interpreter trainee Keum Seo-hee.

904
00:54:53,206 --> 00:54:54,624
Good to see you again.

905
00:54:57,794 --> 00:55:00,088
I'll give it my all, sir.

906
00:55:00,171 --> 00:55:02,424
It will be an honor to serve you.

907
00:55:10,682 --> 00:55:11,683
Yamada.

908
00:55:16,479 --> 00:55:18,189
It's my schedule for next week.

909
00:55:18,273 --> 00:55:20,066
- Have a look.
- Yes, sir.

910
00:55:22,736 --> 00:55:24,029
It's your first day.

911
00:55:25,363 --> 00:55:27,198
Did you get a tour
of the Government-General?

912
00:55:33,371 --> 00:55:36,207
This is the Governor-General's Office,
that's the main conference room.

913
00:55:37,375 --> 00:55:38,209
Main…

914
00:55:38,918 --> 00:55:40,170
This is the supply room.

915
00:55:40,253 --> 00:55:42,297
Sorry? Supply…

916
00:55:42,380 --> 00:55:43,757
INSPECTOR GENERAL'S OFFICE

917
00:55:43,840 --> 00:55:46,092
This is the Inspector General's Office.

918
00:55:46,176 --> 00:55:47,302
Inspector General…

919
00:55:48,386 --> 00:55:49,262
Hurry up.

920
00:56:00,357 --> 00:56:01,816
Have you never seen an elevator?

921
00:56:41,523 --> 00:56:43,191
LADIES' ROOM

922
00:56:43,274 --> 00:56:44,818
The ladies' room is here.

923
00:56:56,746 --> 00:56:57,580
NO ENTRY

924
00:57:16,266 --> 00:57:20,061
I'm following this plan
because I trust you, not her.

925
00:57:20,645 --> 00:57:23,606
I don't completely trust her either.
And I shouldn't.

926
00:57:24,315 --> 00:57:26,192
So we should keep Suzuki's identity,

927
00:57:26,276 --> 00:57:28,778
or that we're a fake married couple,
to ourselves.

928
00:57:29,487 --> 00:57:31,698
Okay, but she'll definitely put us
in danger.

929
00:57:33,074 --> 00:57:34,367
Probably.

930
00:57:35,743 --> 00:57:37,579
But Ga-kyung will do fine.

931
00:57:37,662 --> 00:57:39,873
I'm talking about you, not her.

932
00:57:45,295 --> 00:57:47,922
To be honest, I don't care
what happens to her.

933
00:57:48,006 --> 00:57:50,508
What matters to me is you, So-ran.

934
00:57:57,557 --> 00:58:00,560
Dae-seong!

935
00:58:01,519 --> 00:58:02,479
It's time to eat!

936
00:58:02,562 --> 00:58:03,938
- Okay!
- Okay!

937
00:58:06,357 --> 00:58:08,735
You're supposed to hurry home
when it's time.

938
00:58:22,332 --> 00:58:24,584
What did Ga-kyung give us?

939
00:58:24,667 --> 00:58:26,461
QUICK RELIEF FOR KIDS' CONSTIPATION

940
00:58:26,544 --> 00:58:28,171
I don't know what it says.

941
00:58:36,763 --> 00:58:38,139
And what's this drawing?

942
00:58:39,390 --> 00:58:40,225
Feed the flowers

943
00:58:41,434 --> 00:58:42,602
with manure?

944
00:58:42,685 --> 00:58:44,437
Ga-kyung.

945
00:58:45,021 --> 00:58:47,106
Don't go.

946
00:58:56,491 --> 00:58:58,618
Did Ga-kyung really go to America?

947
00:58:58,701 --> 00:59:00,203
Without even telling us?

948
00:59:07,502 --> 00:59:09,671
Did she think I'd be happy getting a bike?

949
00:59:10,547 --> 00:59:12,006
She could've at least said goodbye.

950
00:59:14,926 --> 00:59:16,594
She shouldn't have left at all.

951
00:59:34,195 --> 00:59:35,947
Get the inspector general's schedule.

952
01:00:01,973 --> 01:00:04,267
Excuse me,
I'm not going to steal anything.

953
01:00:05,476 --> 01:00:08,855
Have you heard the saying
that a guilty conscience needs no accuser?

954
01:00:16,613 --> 01:00:17,739
I'll see you tomorrow.

955
01:00:40,637 --> 01:00:42,847
Why bother locking it?
It's pointless anyway.

956
01:00:55,860 --> 01:00:56,694
There.

957
01:01:08,164 --> 01:01:10,416
Whatever. It's not even funny.

958
01:01:21,678 --> 01:01:22,804
I'm not stealing it.

959
01:01:22,887 --> 01:01:24,931
I'm just going to copy it.

960
01:01:25,014 --> 01:01:26,933
INSPECTOR GENERAL'S SCHEDULE

961
01:01:32,480 --> 01:01:33,773
What? She likes me?

962
01:01:35,024 --> 01:01:37,151
It must be nice to say that so easily.

963
01:02:00,591 --> 01:02:03,177
<i>Give it to the man</i>
<i>with the lemon in his teacup.</i>

964
01:02:05,513 --> 01:02:07,473
The coffee is on the house.

965
01:02:08,766 --> 01:02:11,102
The bill says it's on the house.

966
01:02:17,150 --> 01:02:19,819
Thank you. <i>Arigato.</i>

967
01:02:20,945 --> 01:02:22,864
Right back at you. <i>Arigato.</i>

968
01:02:23,823 --> 01:02:24,949
Come visit more often.

969
01:02:35,001 --> 01:02:36,586
That was easy.

970
01:02:43,384 --> 01:02:44,343
Freeze.

971
01:02:46,053 --> 01:02:47,889
You're a spy, aren't you?

972
01:02:50,475 --> 01:02:51,476
Keum Seo-hee.

973
01:02:52,518 --> 01:02:54,479
No, I mean, Lee Ga-kyung.

974
01:03:45,321 --> 01:03:49,242
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

975
01:03:51,452 --> 01:03:53,079
<i>Don't worry about her.</i>

976
01:03:53,162 --> 01:03:54,539
She's desperate to go to America.

977
01:03:54,622 --> 01:03:56,040
Who's behind this?

978
01:03:56,123 --> 01:03:57,750
<i>So she won't betray us easily.</i>

979
01:03:58,668 --> 01:04:01,128
Your wrists, the bruises, and ankles.

980
01:04:01,212 --> 01:04:02,922
<i>Did he do that to you?</i>

981
01:04:03,005 --> 01:04:05,258
<i>According to the schedule you copied,</i>

982
01:04:05,341 --> 01:04:06,801
<i>at 7:00 p.m. on Monday…</i>

983
01:04:06,884 --> 01:04:09,303
<i>Joseon women are bound to betray us.</i>

984
01:04:09,387 --> 01:04:11,597
<i>She's the one who copied the schedule.</i>

985
01:04:12,181 --> 01:04:13,891
Don't let Mr. Yoo find out.

986
01:04:13,975 --> 01:04:16,310
We have to seize the opportunity
when it comes.

987
01:04:16,394 --> 01:04:19,605
<i>If you put her in danger,</i>
<i>you won't be safe either.</i>

988
01:04:27,321 --> 01:04:29,323
Subtitle translation by: Youji Kim
